+# Pre-ship review — USDC Payouts dApp (Base)
+
+Reviewed: `packages/nextjs` frontend and its configuration, plus the contract registration
+it depends on. Scope was read-only; no code was changed.
+
+Headline: the `/payouts` page works when a user is already connected to Base with an
+allowance in place, and falls over in most other states. There are 13 ship-blocking issues,
+the worst of which (USDC registration) breaks the page on the next `yarn deploy`.
+
+---
+
+## Ship-blocking
+
+### 1. USDC is hand-added to the autogenerated `deployedContracts.ts` — the next deploy wipes it
+
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/contracts/deployedContracts.ts:50` (and `packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:11`)
+
+`USDC` was pasted into the `8453` block of a file whose header says "This file is
+autogenerated by Scaffold-ETH. You should not edit it manually or your changes might be
+overwritten." The next `yarn deploy` regenerates it from deployment artifacts and drops the
+USDC entry. At that point `useScaffoldReadContract({ contractName: "USDC" })` silently
+returns nothing (balance renders as `0`), `deployedContracts[8453].USDC` becomes a type
+error, and the approve flow loses its address. External contracts are not a deploy artifact
+and do not survive this file.
+
+**Fix:** move the entry to `externalContracts.ts`, which is currently an empty stub:
+
+```typescript
+// packages/nextjs/contracts/externalContracts.ts
+import { GenericContractsDeclaration } from "~~/utils/scaffold-eth/contract";
+
+const externalContracts = {
+  8453: {
+    USDC: {
+      address: "0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913",
+      abi: [/* the ABI currently in deployedContracts.ts:52-120 */],
+    },
+  },
+} as const;
+
+export default externalContracts satisfies GenericContractsDeclaration;
+```
+
+Then revert `deployedContracts.ts` to its generated state (Payouts only) and confirm
+`yarn next:check-types` still passes. Once registered there, drop the
+`deployedContracts[8453].USDC` import in `page.tsx` entirely — see issue 4, the scaffold
+write hook resolves the address and ABI by `contractName`.
+
+### 2. Disconnected users get a sentence instead of a Connect button
+
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:70-71`
+
+```tsx
+{!isConnected ? (
+  <p className="mt-4">Please connect your wallet to send a payout.</p>
+```
+
+The page's entire disconnected state is a paragraph telling the user to go find the
+connect control in the header. The action slot should be the action.
+
+**Fix:** render the real button in place of the paragraph:
+
+```tsx
+import { RainbowKitCustomConnectButton } from "~~/components/scaffold-eth";
+// ...
+{!isConnected ? <RainbowKitCustomConnectButton /> : ( ... )}
+```
+
+### 3. No wrong-network gating on the CTA — users can sign against the wrong chain
+
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:99-114`
+
+The only wrong-network handling in the app is the header's `WrongNetworkDropdown`. A user
+connected to, say, Ethereum mainnet sees a fully live "Approve" / "Send Payout" pair, clicks
+it, and hits a wagmi chain-mismatch error that (per issue 5) is swallowed to the console.
+Nothing on screen changes. This is the most likely way a real user loses confidence in the
+app on day one.
+
+**Fix:** branch the primary action on chain id before rendering Approve/Send:
+
+```tsx
+const { address: connectedAddress, isConnected, chain } = useAccount();
+const { targetNetwork } = useTargetNetwork();
+const { switchChain } = useSwitchChain();
+const isWrongNetwork = isConnected && chain?.id !== targetNetwork.id;
+// ...
+{isWrongNetwork ? (
+  <button className="btn btn-primary" onClick={() => switchChain({ chainId: targetNetwork.id })}>
+    Switch to {targetNetwork.name}
+  </button>
+) : ( /* Approve / Send */ )}
+```
+
+### 4. Writes use raw wagmi `useWriteContract` instead of `useScaffoldWriteContract`
+
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:21, 38-43, 51-56`
+
+`useWriteContract().writeContractAsync` resolves as soon as the wallet returns a hash — it
+does not wait for the receipt. So `handlePay` clears the form (lines 57-58) while the
+transaction is still pending, and a transaction that later reverts on-chain leaves the UI
+looking like a success. It also bypasses the scaffold's transaction notifications, block
+explorer links, and network checks.
+
+**Fix:** one scaffold hook per contract, addresses and ABIs resolved by name:
+
+```tsx
+const { writeContractAsync: writeUsdc, isMining: isApproving } = useScaffoldWriteContract({ contractName: "USDC" });
+const { writeContractAsync: writePayouts, isMining: isPaying } = useScaffoldWriteContract({ contractName: "Payouts" });
+
+await writePayouts({ functionName: "pay", args: [recipient, parseUnits(amount, 6), memo] });
+```
+
+This also removes the need to import `deployedContracts` in the page at all.
+
+### 5. Failed and rejected transactions fail silently
+
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:44-46, 59-61`
+
+```tsx
+} catch (e) {
+  console.error(e);
+}
+```
+
+Every failure mode — user rejection, insufficient allowance, insufficient balance, wrong
+chain, RPC error — produces exactly nothing on screen. The user presses "Send Payout",
+nothing happens, and they press it again. For a money-moving app this is the difference
+between a confused user and a double payout.
+
+**Fix:** surface the error next to the action.
+
+```tsx
+import { notification, getParsedError } from "~~/utils/scaffold-eth";
+// ...
+} catch (e) {
+  notification.error(getParsedError(e));
+}
+```
+
+(Moving to `useScaffoldWriteContract` per issue 4 gets most of this for free, but keep an
+explicit catch for anything the hook re-throws.)
+
+### 6. Buttons unlock mid-flight — gated on `isPending`, not `isMining`
+
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:21, 101-102, 108-109`
+
+`isPending` from wagmi drops the moment the wallet hands back a transaction hash, which is
+well before the transaction confirms. Both buttons re-enable while the payout is still in
+the mempool, so a user who sees no feedback (issue 5) can fire a second identical payout.
+
+**Fix:** use `isMining` from `useScaffoldWriteContract`, which is held across
+`waitForTransactionReceipt` and cleared in the hook's `finally` on both confirmation and
+rejection. If the balance/allowance refetch needs a further hold after confirmation, keep
+that in local state and clear it in a `finally` too — never on a `setTimeout`.
+
+### 7. Approve and Send Payout are both live at all times; allowance is never read
+
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:99-114`
+
+There is no `allowance` read anywhere in the page, despite the ABI exposing one. The user is
+shown two equally-weighted buttons with no indication of which one they need. Pressing
+"Send Payout" first — the obvious choice, it is the one that matches the page title —
+reverts in `transferFrom` and, per issue 5, shows nothing.
+
+**Fix:** read the allowance and render exactly one primary action:
+
+```tsx
+const { data: allowance } = useScaffoldReadContract({
+  contractName: "USDC",
+  functionName: "allowance",
+  args: [connectedAddress, payoutsAddress],
+});
+const needsApproval = allowance === undefined || allowance < parseUnits(amount || "0", 6);
+```
+
+Then: Connect → Switch network → Approve → Send Payout, one at a time.
+
+### 8. Recipient is a raw `<input type="text">` cast straight to an address
+
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:78-84, 55`
+
+```tsx
+<input type="text" placeholder="0x... recipient address" ... />
+// ...
+args: [recipient as `0x${string}`, ...]
+```
+
+No validation, no ENS resolution. `vitalik.eth` never reaches the contract. A typo'd or
+truncated address is cast with `as` — TypeScript is silenced, and the call goes out to be
+rejected by viem or, worse, accepted as a valid-but-wrong address. Money moves to it
+irrecoverably.
+
+**Fix:** use the scaffold component, which handles validation, ENS forward resolution, and
+paste normalization:
+
+```tsx
+import { AddressInput } from "@scaffold-ui/components";
+
+<AddressInput value={recipient} onChange={setRecipient} placeholder="Recipient address or ENS" />
+```
+
+Disable the send button while `!isAddress(recipient)`.
+
+### 9. `fromBlock: 0n` scans all of Base mainnet on every page load
+
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:29-34`
+
+```tsx
+fromBlock: 0n,
+watch: true,
+```
+
+Base is past 33M blocks. Public and rate-limited RPC endpoints cap `eth_getLogs` block
+ranges; this request either errors out or grinds through thousands of paginated calls before
+the feed renders. The "Latest payouts" section will look permanently empty or permanently
+loading in production. The contract's own deployment block is already recorded in the
+generated file.
+
+**Fix:**
+
+```tsx
+fromBlock: BigInt(deployedContracts[8453].Payouts.deployedOnBlock), // 33712450
+```
+
+or hoist that constant. Combine with issue 11's polling fix; `watch: true` at a 30s interval
+is currently re-scanning from genesis on a timer.
+
+### 10. Hardcoded `bg-black` page wrapper breaks the theme
+
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:65`
+
+```tsx
+<div className="min-h-screen bg-black text-white">
+```
+
+This bypasses DaisyUI entirely. Light-mode users get a black page wedged between a light
+header and a light footer, and the header's `SwitchTheme` toggle visibly does nothing on
+this page. The hand-rolled `border-gray-700` on the payout cards (line 121) has the same
+problem in reverse.
+
+**Fix:** `className="min-h-screen bg-base-200 text-base-content"`, and `border-base-300` on
+the cards. If a permanently dark app is actually the design decision, set
+`data-theme="dark"` at the layout root and remove `<SwitchTheme/>` from the footer — but do
+not leave a toggle that lies.
+
+### 11. `pollingInterval: 30000` on an L2
+
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/scaffold.config.ts:24`
+
+Base produces a block every 2 seconds. A 30-second poll means a confirmed payout takes up to
+half a minute to appear in the balance and the feed — the app reads as broken or as having
+dropped the transaction.
+
+**Fix:** drop to the responsive range (`pollingInterval: 3000`), or better, keep polling low
+and drive updates from post-confirmation refetches now that writes go through
+`useScaffoldWriteContract`.
+
+### 12. Production RPC is the shared default Alchemy key with a bare `http()` fallback
+
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/scaffold.config.ts:29-35`, `packages/nextjs/services/web3/wagmiConfig.tsx:20`
+
+`NEXT_PUBLIC_ALCHEMY_API_KEY` is blank in `.env.example` and `rpcOverrides` is empty except
+for a commented-out example, so `alchemyApiKey` falls back to `DEFAULT_ALCHEMY_API_KEY` —
+the key shipped with every SE-2 clone. Because `wagmiConfig` detects the default key it
+deprioritizes it behind `http()`, meaning production traffic mostly lands on viem's bare
+public Base endpoint. Under real user load that rate-limits, and the symptoms are exactly
+the ones users will report as bugs: stale balances, an empty payout feed, failed estimates.
+
+**Fix:** add a Base override backed by a real env var —
+
+```typescript
+rpcOverrides: {
+  [chains.base.id]: process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_RPC_URL as string,
+},
+```
+
+— add `NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_RPC_URL` to `.env.example`, and **verify the variable is actually
+set in the Vercel project settings**, not just referenced in code. Referencing it and
+forgetting to set it produces the same silent fallback this issue describes.
+
+### 13. Default WalletConnect project ID
+
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/scaffold.config.ts:40`
+
+`walletConnectProjectId` falls back to `'3a8170812b534d0ff9d794f19a901d64'`, the SE-2
+template ID, and `NEXT_PUBLIC_WALLET_CONNECT_PROJECT_ID` is blank in `.env.example`. This is
+a shared, rate-limited, someone-else's-dashboard project ID. When it throttles, every mobile
+and WalletConnect user is simply unable to connect.
+
+**Fix:** register a project at cloud.reown.com, set
+`NEXT_PUBLIC_WALLET_CONNECT_PROJECT_ID` in the Vercel env, and confirm it is set before
+shipping.
+
+---
+
+## Should-fix
+
+### 14. The app is still branded as Scaffold-ETH 2 in all five places
+
+- **`packages/nextjs/app/layout.tsx:11-12`** — tab title is `"Scaffold-ETH 2 App"`,
+  description is `"Built with 🏗 Scaffold-ETH 2"`. Fix: `title: "USDC Payouts"`,
+  description describing the product.
+- **`packages/nextjs/utils/scaffold-eth/getMetadata.ts:8`** — `titleTemplate` is
+  `"%s | Scaffold-ETH 2"`, so every sub-page title carries it too.
+- **`packages/nextjs/components/Footer.tsx:50-74`** — "Fork me" → the SE-2 repo, "Built with
+  ♥ at BuidlGuidl", and a "Support" link to a BuidlGuidl Telegram invite. Real users
+  clicking "Support" for a payout problem land in a stranger's chat. Replace with your own
+  support channel or drop the row.
+- **`README.md:1-20`** — still the upstream SE-2 readme; nothing about Payouts, Base, or how
+  to run this app.
+- **`packages/nextjs/public/favicon.png`** — unchanged SE-2 default.
+
+### 15. Home page is still the untouched scaffold placeholder
+
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/app/page.tsx:20-45`
+
+`/` greets users with "Welcome to Scaffold-ETH 2", "Get started by editing
+packages/nextjs/app/page.tsx", and instructions to edit `YourContract.sol`. This is the
+first screen a real user sees. Replace with a short product intro and a link into
+`/payouts`, or redirect `/` to `/payouts`.
+
+### 16. Pill-shaped inputs clip their content
+
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/styles/globals.css:36` and `:63`
+
+`--radius-field: 9999rem` renders fully-rounded inputs, selects, and the textarea. Long
+content — pasted addresses especially — visually clips against the curve.
+
+**Fix:** set `--radius-field: 0.5rem` in **both** `@plugin "daisyui/theme"` blocks (light and
+dark), at theme level rather than patching individual components with `rounded-*`.
+
+### 17. `loading` class on the buttons swallows the label
+
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:101, 108`
+
+```tsx
+className={`btn btn-primary ${isPending ? "loading" : ""}`}
+```
+
+DaisyUI's `loading` on a `btn` replaces the button's content with a spinner. Mid-transaction
+the user sees a bare spinner with no text, so "Approve" and "Send Payout" become
+indistinguishable.
+
+**Fix:** keep the label and add an inline spinner next to it:
+
+```tsx
+<button className="btn btn-primary" disabled={isApproving}>
+  {isApproving && <span className="loading loading-spinner loading-sm" />}
+  Approve
+</button>
+```
+
+### 18. Hand-rolled `shorten()` instead of `<Address/>`
+
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:13, 122, 124`
+
+The payout feed renders truncated hex through a local `shorten()`. No blockie, no ENS name,
+no copy button, no link to Basescan — so a user who wants to check where a payout actually
+went has to select and paste the address by hand, and cannot because it is truncated.
+
+**Fix:** `<Address address={payout.args.to} />` from `@scaffold-ui/components`, and delete
+`shorten`.
+
+### 19. The Payouts contract address is never shown
+
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:64-76`
+
+The page shows the user's own balance but never the contract they are approving and sending
+funds through. Before granting an ERC-20 allowance, a user should be able to see and verify
+the spender.
+
+**Fix:** render `<Address address={payoutsAddress} />` in the header area, labelled
+"Payouts contract".
+
+### 20. Phantom users cannot connect
+
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/services/web3/wagmiConnectors.tsx:20-28`
+
+The wallet list is MetaMask, WalletConnect, Ledger, Base Account, Rainbow, and Safe. Phantom
+has a substantial EVM user base and is not in the list, so those users have no way in.
+
+**Fix:** import `phantomWallet` from `@rainbow-me/rainbowkit/wallets` and add it to
+`wallets`.
+
+### 21. No mobile redirect to the wallet app after a write
+
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:36-62`
+
+On mobile WalletConnect, calling `writeContractAsync` dispatches the signing request but
+leaves the user staring at the browser. They have to know to switch apps by hand to find the
+prompt. Most will assume the button did nothing and tap again.
+
+**Fix:** after firing the write (never before — navigating away first means the write never
+runs), redirect to the connected wallet's deep link. Target the wallet the user actually
+connected with rather than a hardcoded scheme, and skip it when already inside a wallet's
+in-app browser.
+
+### 22. Balance renders `0` while it is still loading
+
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:75`
+
+```tsx
+{usdcBalance !== undefined ? formatUnits(usdcBalance, 6) : "0"}
+```
+
+`undefined` (not loaded, or contract unregistered per issue 1) is displayed identically to a
+genuine zero balance. A user with funds sees "Your balance: 0 USDC" on every page load and
+concludes the app cannot see their money.
+
+**Fix:** render a skeleton or `<Balance/>`-style placeholder for the undefined case.
+Related: line 26 passes `args: [connectedAddress]` where `connectedAddress` may be
+`undefined` — gate the read with `query: { enabled: !!connectedAddress }`.
+
+### 23. No input validation before the write
+
+**File:** `packages/nextjs/app/payouts/page.tsx:85-91, 42, 55`
+
+`parseUnits(amount || "0", 6)` is called on whatever the number input holds.
+`type="number"` accepts `e`/`+`/`-` notation, which makes `parseUnits` throw; more than six
+decimal places is silently truncated; an empty amount sends `0`, which the contract reverts
+on (`Payouts: zero amount`); and nothing checks the amount against the user's balance. Every
+one of these currently surfaces as "nothing happened" per issue 5.
+
+**Fix:** disable the send button unless the recipient is a valid address, the parsed amount
+is greater than zero, and it is not greater than `usdcBalance`. Show the reason inline.
+Also clear `recipient` alongside `amount` and `memo` after a confirmed payout (line 57-58
+clears only two of three).
+
+### 24. Confirm the deployed contract is verified on Basescan
+
+**File:** `packages/hardhat/deploy/00_deploy_payouts.ts`, contract at
+`0x7c3f2A9d41B85E60Cf14a14e2b9D073A55e0b6F2`
+
+I could not check the explorer from here. Before shipping, confirm the source is verified —
+unverified bytecode on a contract users grant USDC allowances to reads as something to hide,
+and it blocks anyone from auditing the `pay` path independently.
+
+**Fix:** `yarn verify --network base`, then load the address on basescan.org and confirm the
+Contract tab shows source.

